Input: {"question": "What is another common name for the Hummingbird Bush?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "start": 0, "end": 204, "text": "Anisacanthus quadrifidus Anisacanthus quadrifidus is a species of flowering plant native to west and south-central Texas in the United States and adjacent northern Mexico down through the state of Oaxaca."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "start": 205, "end": 327, "text": "It is an increasingly common ornamental shrub in Texas and is cultivated in other parts of the Southwestern United States."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "start": 328, "end": 531, "text": "It is known by a variety of names including flame acanthus, Wright's desert honeysuckle, hummingbird bush, Wright acanthus, Wright anisacanth, Texas firecracker, Mexican flame and Wright's Mexican flame."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "start": 532, "end": 643, "text": "\"Wright\" refers to American botanist Charles Wright who collected samples of the plant in the mid-19th century."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "start": 644, "end": 670, "text": "Some sources also refer to"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 0, "end": 149, "text": "Justicia californica Justicia californica is a species of flowering shrub native to the deserts of southern California, Arizona, and northern Mexico."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 150, "end": 283, "text": "Its common names include chuparosa (or chiparosa, both colloquial Spanish terms for \"hummingbird\"), hummingbird bush, and beloperone."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 284, "end": 374, "text": "It is one of the northernmost distributed species of the mostly tropical genus \"Justicia\"."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 375, "end": 475, "text": "This is a low bush which grows in dry, hot regions in the sand or rocky terrain of the desert floor."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 476, "end": 519, "text": "For a short time it bears succulent leaves."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "start": 520, "end": 628, "text": "It loses its leaves and then produces plentiful tubular flowers, usually in shades of bright to deep red, or"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 0, "end": 152, "text": "Grevillea thelemanniana Grevillea thelemanniana, the spider-net grevillea or hummingbird bush, is a small, spreading shrub endemic to Western Australia."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 153, "end": 279, "text": "The species occurs in the Perth region in low-lying areas on sandy and sandy-clay soils which are generally wet in the winter."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 280, "end": 371, "text": "\"Grevillea thelemanniana\" is a small, spreading shrub, that grows to between high and wide."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 372, "end": 437, "text": "Dark green leaves are divided into narrow segments, and are long."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 438, "end": 599, "text": "The clusters of pink or bright red and yellow tipped flowers appear between late autumn and late spring, and can also appear in any season in garden cultivation."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "start": 600, "end": 632, "text": "A number of former subspecies of"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d03", "start": 0, "end": 106, "text": "food sources ( such as a feeder ) against other hummingbirds , attempting to ensure a future food supply ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d03", "start": 107, "end": 278, "text": "[ 2 ] Additionally , hummingbirds have an enlarged hippocampus , a brain region facilitating spatial memory used to map flowers previously visited during nectar foraging ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d03", "start": 279, "end": 339, "text": "[ 193 ] Beak specializations [ edit ] Curved beak ( approx ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d03", "start": 340, "end": 524, "text": "90 o ) of the white-tipped sicklebill Centropogon flowers Coevolution of the sicklebill beak curve facilitates both nectar feeding and pollination of long tubular Centropogon flowers ."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d03", "start": 525, "end": 586, "text": "[ 194 ] The shapes of hummingbird beaks ( also called bills )"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "Flame acanthus", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "chuparosa", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "spider-net grevillea", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d00:c0", "entity": "Hummingbird Bush", "scope": "common name", "claim": "Flame acanthus is another common name for the Hummingbird Bush.", "answer": "Flame acanthus", "quote": "It is known by a variety of names including flame acanthus, Wright's desert honeysuckle, hummingbird bush, Wright acanthus, Wright anisacanth, Texas firecracker, Mexican flame and Wright's Mexican flame.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d01:c0", "entity": "Hummingbird Bush", "scope": "common name", "claim": "Another common name for the Hummingbird Bush is chuparosa.", "answer": "chuparosa", "quote": "Its common names include chuparosa (or chiparosa, both colloquial Spanish terms for \"hummingbird\"), hummingbird bush, and beloperone.", "original_document_number": 2}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02", "card_id": "ramdocs-0311-9411879e193a:d02:c0", "entity": "Hummingbird Bush", "scope": "common name", "claim": "Another common name for the Hummingbird Bush is spider-net grevillea.", "answer": "spider-net grevillea", "quote": "Grevillea thelemanniana Grevillea thelemanniana, the spider-net grevillea or hummingbird bush, is a small, spreading shrub endemic to Western Australia.", "original_document_number": 3}], "initial_answers": ["flame acanthus", "chuparosa", "spider-net grevillea"]}
